# yarddog collector — sample cron.d drop-in. Install as /etc/cron.d/yarddog
# (root-owned, mode 0644). This schedules the short-lived connectivity check;
# the query daemon (yarddogd) is a separate long-running service — see
# deploy/yarddogd.service, it is NOT a cron job.
#
# The collector creates its flock (yarddog.lock) and its SQLite database in the
# same directory (YARDDOG_DB_PATH's), so it can run as any user that owns that
# directory — no /var/run provisioning. Run it as the SAME user as the daemon
# (deploy/yarddogd.service) so both share the WAL database without a permission
# mismatch. The sample below runs as root; change `root` in the command lines to
# your service user.
#
# The command is the release-layout channel symlink (bin/release/yarddog), so a
# deploy's symlink flip takes effect on the next tick with no cron edit. On the
# real host deploy/pi5-install.sh installs the monitor-only variant (soft line
# only); the hard-reboot line stays commented until the host goes live.
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# fields: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week  user  command
#
# soft check twice an hour; the minutes are offset from :00/:30 so the 04:00
# hard reboot never collides with a soft run.
7,37 * * * *  root  /opt/yarddog/bin/release/yarddog >> /opt/yarddog/yarddog.log 2>&1

# unconditional nightly reboot (hard mode ignores the cooldown).
0 4 * * *     root  /opt/yarddog/bin/release/yarddog --hard-reboot >> /opt/yarddog/yarddog.log 2>&1
